Belgian law does not prohibit hitchhiking; it prohibits pedestrians on the fast roads. Under [https://www.ejustice.just.fgov.be/eli/arrete/1975/12/01/1975120130/justel article 21.1 of the ''code de la route'' / ''wegcode''], access to an ''autoroute'' (''autosnelweg'') is forbidden to pedestrians, cyclists, mopeds and animals, and vehicles may enter or leave it only at the places built for that — so the carriageway, the hard shoulder and the slip road are all out. Article 21.4.4° lets drivers stop or park only on the parking areas signposted E9a, which is where a hitchhiker asks for the next ride, and article 22.1 reserves ''routes pour automobiles'' (''autowegen'') for motor vehicles too. Everywhere else hitchhiking is unregulated and legal: thumb from the verge before the motorway sign, or at the last service area before it. On 1 September 2026 the 1975 code gives way to the new ''Code de la voie publique'', whose article 24 §1 carries exactly the same ban on pedestrians.
